  "verses": {
    "verse": [
      [
        "Thou art the King of Israel,",
        "thou David's royal Son,",
        "who in the Lord's name comest,",
        "the King and Blessed One."
      ],
      [
        "The company of angels",
        "are praising Thee on high,",
        "and mortal men, and all things",
        "created, make reply."
      ],
      [
        "The people of the Hebrews",
        "with palms before Thee went;",
        "our praise, and prayer, and anthems",
        "before Thee we present."
      ],
      [
        "In hastening to Thy Passion,",
        "they raised their hymns of praise;",
        "in reigning 'midst Thy glory,",
        "our melody we raise."
      ],
      [
        "Thou didst accept their praises;",
        "accept the prayers we bring,",
        "who in all good delightest,",
        "Thou good and gracious King!"
      ]
    ]
  },
  "arrangerNotes": "Verse 1 (Palm Sunday hymn), soprano (melody) line, verified against the Mutopia LilyPond score. 'Israel' is sung in three syllables; a slurred pair carries one syllable in 'comest'. No simplification needed. D drum: as written (range 1 to high 3), D minLayout 13. C drum: the C drum has no high 2/3, so line 2 ('to thee, Redeemer, King') and lines 4–5 ('made sweet hosannas ring. Thou art the King of Israel') are played one octave lower; C minLayout 11. C and D arrangements differ where noted above (each fits its own drum). Lyrics of the added verses: Verses 2–5: John Mason Neale's translation for the Hymnal Noted, as printed in his Mediæval Hymns and Sequences (1867), from Wikisource (https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mediaeval_Hymns_and_Sequences/Gloria,_laus,_et_honor). Sung as refrain, verse, refrain, … ending with the refrain.",
  "verified": true
}
